{{also|compétent|compètent}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|competent}}, borrowed from {{bor|en|fro|competent}}, from {{der|en|la|competens|competens, competentem}}, present participle of {{m|la|competō||coincide, be equal to, be capable of}}. Compare Dutch {{m|nl|competent||competent}}, German {{m|de|kompetent||competent}}, Danish {{m|da|kompetent||competent}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en-us-competent.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}
* {{IPA|/ˈkɒmpətənt/|lang=en}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Having sufficient [[skill]], [[knowledge]], [[ability]], or [[qualifications]].
#: ''He is a '''competent''' skier and an expert snowboarder.''
#* {{quote-book|lang=en|passage=I believe in that myself because it has been explained by '''competent''' men as the convolutions of the grey matter.| author={{w|James Joyce}}| title=''{{w|Ulysses}}''| year=1922}}
#* {{quote-book|lang=en|passage=That as a '''competent''' keyless citizen he had proceeded energetically from the unknown to the known through the incertitude of the void.| author={{w|James Joyce}}| title=''{{w|Ulysses}}''| year=1922}}
# {{lb|en|legal}} Having [[jurisdiction]] or [[authority]] over a particular [[issue]] or [[question]].
#: ''For any disagreements arising from this contract, the '''competent''' court shall be the Springfield Circuit Court.''
#: ''judicial authority having '''competent''' jurisdiction''
# [[adequate|Adequate]] for the [[purpose]]
#* '''1662''', {{w|Henry More}}, ''{{S|An Antidote Against Atheism}}'', Book II, A Collection of Several Philosophical Writings of Dr. Henry More, p. 67:
#*: "For if [birds] had been ''Viviparous'', the burthen of their womb, if they had brought forth any '''competent''' number at a time, had been so big and heavy, that their wings would have failed them {{...}}"
# {{lb|en|biology|of a cell wall}} [[permeable]] to [[foreign]] [[DNA]]

